# Implement rb_validate in src/rbtree.c

## Drafts

```c
static int rb_validate_node(const rbtree_t *t, const rbnode_t *x,
                             const char *lo, const char *hi, int *bh)
{
    if (x == t->nil) {
        *bh = 0;
        return 1;
    }

    if (lo && strcmp(x->key, lo) <= 0) return 0;
    if (hi && strcmp(x->key, hi) >= 0) return 0;

    if (x->color == RB_RED &&
        (x->left->color == RB_RED || x->right->color == RB_RED))
        return 0;

    int bhL, bhR;
    if (!rb_validate_node(t, x->left,  lo,     x->key, &bhL)) return 0;
    if (!rb_validate_node(t, x->right, x->key, hi,     &bhR)) return 0;
    if (bhL != bhR) return 0;

    *bh = bhL + (x->color == RB_BLACK ? 1 : 0);
    return 1;
}

int rb_validate(const rbtree_t *t)
{
    if (t->root->color != RB_BLACK)
        return -1;

    int bh;
    return rb_validate_node(t, t->root, NULL, NULL, &bh) ? 0 : -1;
}
```

### Where each variable's value comes from, frame by frame

Every call to `rb_validate_node` gets its own stack frame with its own `x`, `lo`, `hi`, `bhL`, `bhR`. Nothing is shared or global — the only thing that crosses frames is what's passed as an argument or written through a pointer.

- **`x`** — passed in by the caller. At the root call it's `t->root`; every deeper call it's `x->left` or `x->right` from the *parent* frame, i.e. "the node I'm about to validate."
- **`lo`, `hi`** — the open bound this node's key must fall inside, also handed down by the parent frame: `x`'s left child is called with `(lo, x->key)` (still bounded below by whatever bounded `x`, newly bounded above by `x`'s own key), and its right child with `(x->key, hi)` (mirror). This is *threading*, not a fixed parent-only check — a node four levels down still carries the bound set by an ancestor two levels up if nothing tighter appeared in between. That's what catches the deep BST-order violation (a node that's fine vs. its immediate parent but violates a grandparent's bound).
- **`bhL`, `bhR`** — local `int`s declared fresh in *this* frame, at the point they're needed. They are never assigned directly by this frame; they're filled in as a side effect of the recursive calls: `&bhL` is passed as the callee's `bh` parameter, so when the callee eventually executes `*bh = ...`, it is writing through that pointer into this frame's `bhL`. The callee's `return` value (1/0) and its write to `*bh` are two separate channels — the write only becomes trustworthy if the return was 1, which is why the code checks `if (!rb_validate_node(...)) return 0;` before ever reading `bhL`.
- **`*bh`** (this frame's own out-parameter) — computed only after both children have reported back and agreed (`bhL == bhR`). It's the black-height of the subtree rooted at `x`, handed back to *this frame's own caller* the same way `bhL`/`bhR` were handed to it — recursion one level up sees this frame's `*bh` write land in *its* `bhL` or `bhR`.

### How black-height escalates up the call stack

Black-height isn't computed top-down; it's assembled bottom-up and only becomes meaningful once it reaches a point where two sibling subtrees can be compared:

1. At every `nil` leaf, the base case fires and reports `bh = 0` — this is the common floor every path starts counting from.
2. A frame one level up receives `bh = 0` from *both* its (nil) children as `bhL` and `bhR`, confirms they're equal, and reports its own `*bh = 0 + (its own color == BLACK ? 1 : 0)` upward — i.e. a leaf node's black-height contribution appears for the first time only once its parent adds it in.
3. Each subsequent frame up the stack repeats the same move: take the two `bh` values it just received from below (already fully accounting for everything under them), check they match, then add `1` iff *this* node itself is black, and pass that single combined number further up as its own `*bh`.
4. By the time the recursion unwinds back to the call on `t->root`, the `bh` written there would be the black-height of the whole tree — except `rb_validate` never reads it, because there's no grandparent left to compare it against. The comparisons that matter (`bhL != bhR`) all already happened lower in the stack, once per node, each checking only its own two children's already-aggregated counts. No frame ever needs to know the black-height of anything outside its own subtree.

So a black-height mismatch anywhere in the tree is caught exactly once, at the lowest common ancestor of the two unequal paths — by the time that check runs, both sides have already folded in everything beneath them.

## Context

`rb_validate` in [src/rbtree.c](src/rbtree.c#L94-L100) is currently a stub that unconditionally returns `-1`. `tests/test_rbtree.c` already contains `test_rb_validate` (lines 183-362), a white-box suite that builds structurally-valid-but-invariant-violating trees directly (bypassing `rb_insert`, which isn't implemented yet either) and asserts `rb_validate` catches each violation. That test file is the authoritative spec here — it confirms exactly the four invariants discussed earlier in this session: key order, red-implies-black-children, uniform black-height, and root-is-black. This plan implements `rb_validate` to satisfy that suite.

## Approach

Replace the stub body of `rb_validate` and add one static recursive helper above it in [src/rbtree.c](src/rbtree.c), no header changes (forbidden by CLAUDE.md).

**`rb_validate_node`** handles three invariants for the subtree rooted at `x`, in this order, using short names instead of I1/I2/I3:
- **order** — `x`'s key falls strictly inside the `(lo, hi)` bound threaded down from ancestors.
- **no-red-red** — if `x` is red, neither child is red.
- **black-height** — both children report equal black-height; `x` folds in `+1` iff it's black itself, and reports that upward.

**`rb_validate`** handles the fourth invariant directly, since it's a property of the root alone and doesn't compose subtree-by-subtree:
- **black-root** — `t->root->color == RB_BLACK`, checked once before recursing. This also correctly passes the empty-tree case, since `t->root == t->nil` and `nil` is black by construction ([rbtree.c:43](src/rbtree.c#L43)).

No allocation occurs, so the goto-cleanup pattern doesn't apply. Recursion depth is bounded by tree height (O(log n) once `rb_insert`'s fixup is real). Compiles clean under C23 / `-Wall -Wextra -Werror` (no VLAs, no unused params).

## Verification

- `test_rb_validate` in `tests/test_rbtree.c` exercises this directly (11 cases: 4 red-red orientations, 4 order violations including 2 deep, 2 black-height, 1 root-color) and should pass standalone once this lands.
- Caveat: `make test` builds a single binary whose `main()` also calls `test_rb_create` and `test_rb_insert`; `rb_insert` is still a stub, so the full `make test` run will still fail/abort at `test_rb_insert` — that's expected and out of scope for this change. I'll compile and run to confirm `test_rb_validate`'s assertions specifically pass (it runs first in `main`), and show the output, rather than claiming a full green `make test`/`make asan`/`make memcheck` for this commit.

